Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. BOY'S NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A warm, period live-action nursery glows beside a coal fire. In a stocking hangs RABBIT, fat and bunchy, with a spotted brown-and-white velveteen coat, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, boot-button eyes, and holly between his paws.

(SOUND: fire crackles, paper rustles, and a nursery clock ticks.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a Velveteen Rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

A small, fair-haired BOY in a white nightshirt pulls Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him with delight.

(SOUND: the Boy laughs; a toy engine whistles.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Rabbit was quite the best of all.

Tissue paper and new presents soon fill the floor. The Boy reaches for the toy engine, leaving Rabbit tipped gently against the stocking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him, and then, in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. BOY'S NURSERY - NIGHT

Rabbit lies on the nursery floor beneath a toy cupboard. A clockwork mouse and painted model boat face away from him, while an old SKIN HORSE rests near the fender.

(SOUND: clockwork gears click down; wind brushes the windowpane.)

Skin Horse is brown and bald in patches, with seams showing through his coat and only a few hairs left in his tail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The firelight catches the Skin Horse's worn seams.

SKIN HORSE
That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

Rabbit stares at his bright whiskers and smooth pink ears.

(SOUND: a floorboard creaks in the hall.)

INT. BOY'S NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, broad-shouldered in a dark dress and white apron, rummages impatiently through the toy cupboard. The fair-haired Boy waits in bed, unable to find his china dog.

(SOUND: cupboard door bangs; rain taps the nursery glass.)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana drags Rabbit out by one ear and drops him into the Boy's arms.

The Boy curls around Rabbit beneath the blankets. Rabbit is squeezed tight, then tucked under the pillow, then drawn beneath the Boy's warm chin.

(SOUND: the night-light hisses softly; the Boy's breathing slows.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very soon he grew to like it, for the Boy used to talk to him, and made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes that he said were like the burrows the real rabbits lived in.

Night after night passes in the same bed. Rabbit's pink nose rubs pale, one thread whisker loosens, and his tail begins to come unsewn.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit would snuggle down close under his little warm chin and dream, with the Boy's hands clasped close round him all night long.

EXT. HOUSE GARDEN - SPRING DUSK

Dew shines on the lawn and flower beds. Rabbit, now a little dusty and worn, lies half-buried in a burrow the Boy has made beneath raspberry canes.

(SOUND: blackbirds call; a distant gate shuts.)

The fair-haired Boy is called away from the garden. He runs toward the house and leaves Rabbit behind.

Dusk deepens. Nana crosses the wet lawn carrying a candle, her white apron dark against the garden shadows.

(SOUND: grass brushes Nana's skirt; the candle flame sputters.)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy sits upright in bed beyond the open nursery window and reaches both hands toward Rabbit.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Rabbit rests against the Boy's chest. His dulled boot-button eyes seem suddenly wise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

EXT. WOOD BEHIND THE HOUSE - SUMMER EVENING

Bracken glows in the last light. The fair-haired Boy, in a blue wool jacket, makes Rabbit a nest under the fronds before wandering away to pick flowers.

(SOUND: insects hum; branches stir above the wood.)

Rabbit's fur is threadbare, his brown spots faded, and his pink ear lining grey. Ants travel between his velvet paws.

Two living rabbits creep from the bracken. One, a sleek brown WILD RABBIT with bright eyes and long whiskers, noses close to Rabbit.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

Wild Rabbit hops sideways and balances on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

Rabbit stays very still, his back made all in one piece.

RABBIT
I don't want to!

Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and peers beneath Rabbit.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Wild Rabbit whirls and dances in the bracken. Rabbit watches, longing to join him.

RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

Wild Rabbit's whiskers brush Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps back.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps pound through the trees. The Boy runs past the nest.

(SOUND: running feet; a sudden stamp and rush of small bodies through fern.)

The wild rabbits vanish in flashes of white tail.

RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The bracken sways where they passed. Rabbit is alone.

INT. BOY'S NURSERY - NIGHT

The same fair-haired Boy lies flushed and trembling beneath blankets, his face bright with fever. Rabbit, now shabby and nearly shapeless, is clasped tightly beneath his chin.

(SOUND: a bedside clock ticks; the Boy breathes unevenly.)

Nana, still in her dark dress and white apron, tends a lamp while shadowy visitors' footsteps cross the hall outside.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went in the nursery, and a light burned all night.

Rabbit remains hidden under the bedclothes as the Boy twists in sleep.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

The fever drags through the night. The Boy's hot hand presses Rabbit against his cheek.

(SOUND: wind rattles the window; the lamp flame flutters.)

Morning light reaches the bed. The Boy's fever breaks. Days later, he sits up with a picture book while Rabbit cuddles at his side.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He snuggled down patiently, and looked forward to the time when the Boy should be well again, and they would go out in the garden among the flowers and the butterflies and play splendid games in the raspberry thicket like they used to.

EXT. NURSERY BALCONY - SUNNY MORNING

The recovered Boy, pale but smiling beneath a shawl, sits in a wicker chair on the balcony. Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes, one worn ear poking out.

(SOUND: sparrows chatter; a distant carriage rolls along the road.)

A grey-bearded DOCTOR in a black coat closes his case. Nana stands beside a packed seaside trunk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything was arranged, and now it only remained to carry out the doctor's orders.

Rabbit sees the open sky and the trunk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah! To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!

Nana notices Rabbit in the blankets.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

DOCTOR
What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

(SOUND: the doctor's case snaps shut.)

Rabbit lies motionless as Nana gathers the bedclothes around him.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

A sack of old picture books and rubbish lies behind the fowl-house. Rabbit wriggles his head through its untied opening, shivering under a thin, threadbare coat.

(SOUND: fowls rustle in their coop; night wind hisses through raspberry canes.)

Moonlight falls across the garden where Rabbit once played. The raspberry thicket stands nearby, tall and close.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
While the Boy was asleep, dreaming of the seaside, the little Rabbit lay among the old picture-books in the corner behind the fowl-house, and he felt very lonely.

Rabbit looks toward the dark flower bed and the distant wood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear slips down Rabbit's shabby velvet nose and drops into the soil.

(SOUND: the tear lands; roots softly stir beneath the earth.)

An emerald-leaved flower pushes up where the tear fell. Its golden cup opens.

A FAIRY steps from the blossom. She wears pearl and dew-drops, flowers in her hair and around her neck, and carries a soft, radiant light.

(SOUND: a faint bell-like shimmer rises from the flower.)

The Fairy lifts Rabbit and kisses his damp velvet nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

RABBIT
No.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy holds Rabbit close and rises over the fowl-house toward the moonlit wood.

EXT. WOOD BEHIND THE HOUSE - MOONLIT NIGHT

Silver moonlight frosts the bracken. Wild rabbits dance in an open glade, then stop in a ring as the Fairy descends with Rabbit in her arms.

(SOUND: soft paws patter on grass; leaves whisper in the night breeze.)

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

The Fairy lowers Rabbit onto velvet grass and kisses him once more.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

Rabbit sits rigidly among the wild rabbits, ashamed of the one-piece back he remembers.

Something tickles his nose. Rabbit lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: a quick intake of breath; grass blades brush against new fur.)

Rabbit freezes. Brown fur, soft and shiny, covers him. His ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers sweep the grass. Strong hind legs gather beneath him.

(SOUND: Rabbit's first leap thumps the turf; the wild rabbits answer with racing paws.)

Rabbit springs sideways, whirls, and races in joy with the others.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

When Rabbit turns to find the Fairy, only moonlight and the opened flower remain.

EXT. WOOD BEHIND THE HOUSE - SPRING DAY

Spring sunlight warms the familiar bracken. The fair-haired Boy, strong again and wearing his blue wool jacket, plays among the trees.

(SOUND: birdsong fills the wood; twigs snap under the Boy's shoes.)

Two rabbits creep from the bracken and watch him. One is brown all over. The other is RABBIT, small and sleek, with shining brown fur and faint old spots showing beneath it.

Rabbit pauses, his round black eyes fixed on the Boy who first loved him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Boy smiles at the rabbit, then runs on through the sunlit wood. Rabbit watches him go before turning back to the wild rabbits.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
